# =============================================================================
# Lv-00 Axiom Package: Non-Well-Founded Set Theory (AFA — Aczel's Anti-Foundation Axiom)
# =============================================================================
#
# Corresponding Mathematical Theory:
#   Non-well-founded set theory (hyperset theory), formalized by replacing
#   the Axiom of Regularity (Foundation) in ZF with Aczel's Anti-Foundation
#   Axiom (AFA). This allows sets to contain themselves and form infinite
#   descending membership chains, while preserving the axiom of extensionality
#   strengthened by bisimulation equivalence.
#
# References:
#   - Aczel, P. (1988). "Non-Well-Founded Sets." CSLI Lecture Notes, vol. 14.
#     Stanford University. ISBN 0-937073-22-9.
#     https://archive.org/details/nonwellfoundedse0000acze/page/
#   - Barwise, J. & Moss, L.S. (1996). "Vicious Circles: On the Mathematics
#     of Non-Wellfounded Phenomena." CSLI Publications. ISBN 1-57586-009-0.
#   - Forti, M. & Honsell, F. (1983). "Set theory with free construction
#     principles." Annali della Scuola Normale Superiore di Pisa, 10(3): 493-522.
#   - Wikipedia: Non-well-founded set theory
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-well-founded_set_theory
#   - Wikipedia: Aczel's anti-foundation axiom
#     https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aczel%27s_anti-foundation_axiom
#   - Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy: Non-wellfounded Set Theory
#     https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nonwellfounded-set-theory/
#   - Moss, L.S. (2018). "Non-wellfounded Set Theory." Stanford Encyclopedia
#     of Philosophy (Summer 2018 ed.).
#   - Mirimanoff, D. (1917). "Les antinomies de Russell et de Burali-Forti
#     et le probleme fondamental de la theorie des ensembles."
#     L'Enseignement Mathematique, 19: 37-52.
#   - Finsler, P. (1926). "Uber die Grundlagen der Mengenlehre. I."
#     Math. Z., 25: 683-713.
#   - Scott, D. (1960). "A different kind of model for set theory."
#     Unpublished talk, 1960 Stanford Congress.
#   - Boffa, M. (1968). "Les ensembles extraordinaires."
#     Bulletin de la Societe Mathematique de Belgique, 20: 3-15.
#   - Sangiorgi, D. (2011). "Origins of bisimulation and coinduction."
#     In: Advanced Topics in Bisimulation and Coinduction, Cambridge Univ. Press.
#
# Formal Language:
#   Primitive notions:
#     - Hyperset (a "set" that may violate well-foundedness)
#     - ∈ (membership, binary predicate)
#     - = (equality, defined via strong extensionality with bisimulation)
#
#   The universe of hypersets extends the von Neumann cumulative hierarchy V.
#   Every well-founded set (element of V) is also a hyperset, but the hyperset
#   universe additionally contains non-well-founded sets such as:
#     - Quine atom: Ω = {Ω}  (the unique solution to x = {x} under AFA)
#     - Infinite descending chains: a₀ ∋ a₁ ∋ a₂ ∋ ...
#     - Mutual recursion: a = {b}, b = {a}
#
# Core Axioms (ZF - Regularity + AFA):
#   I.   Axiom of Extensionality (strengthened):
#        ∀x∀y[∀z(z∈x ↔ z∈y) → x=y]
#        Under AFA, this is equivalent to: two hypersets are equal iff
#        they are bisimilar (their decoration graphs are isomorphic).
#
#   II.  Anti-Foundation Axiom (AFA):
#        Every accessible pointed graph (apg) has a unique decoration.
#        Equivalently: every system of equations of the form
#          x_i = {x_j : j ∈ I_i}  (for i ∈ I)
#        has a unique solution (up to isomorphism of the index set).
#        This replaces the Axiom of Regularity.
#
#   III. Axiom Schema of Specification (Separation): [retained from ZF]
#        ∀z∀w₁…∀wₙ∃y∀x[x∈y ↔ (x∈z ∧ φ(x,w₁,…,wₙ))]
#
#   IV.  Axiom of Pairing: [retained from ZF]
#        ∀x∀y∃z[x∈z ∧ y∈z]
#
#   V.   Axiom of Union: [retained from ZF]
#        ∀F∃A∀Y∀x[(x∈Y ∧ Y∈F) → x∈A]
#
#   VI.  Axiom Schema of Replacement: [retained from ZF]
#        ∀A∀w₁…∀wₙ[∀x(x∈A → ∃!y φ(x,y,w₁,…,wₙ))
#        → ∃B∀y(y∈B ↔ ∃x(x∈A ∧ φ(x,y,w₁,…,wₙ)))]
#
#   VII. Axiom of Infinity: [retained from ZF]
#        ∃I[∅∈I ∧ ∀x(x∈I → x∪{x}∈I)]
#
#   VIII.Axiom of Power Set: [retained from ZF]
#        ∀x∃y∀z[z⊆x → z∈y]
#
#   IX.  Axiom of Choice: [retained from ZFC]
#        ∀X[∅∉X → ∃f:X→∪X such that ∀A∈X, f(A)∈A]
#
# Key Concepts:
#   - Accessible Pointed Graph (apg): a directed graph with exactly one
#     designated node (the "point") such that every node is reachable
#     from the point via a finite path.
#   - Decoration: a labeling of an apg's nodes with hypersets such that
#     each node's label is exactly the set of labels of its children.
#   - Bisimulation: a relation R between two hypersets a, b such that
#     for every x∈a there exists y∈b with xRy, and vice versa.
#     Under AFA, bisimilarity coincides with equality.
#   - Solution Lemma: every system of set equations has a unique solution.
#     This is the operational form of AFA.
#
# Relationship to Other Anti-Foundation Axioms:
#   Four major anti-foundation axioms exist, with increasing universe sizes:
#     AFA (Aczel/Forti-Honsell): V ⊆ A — based on apg decoration
#     SAFA (Scott): A ⊆ S — based on smooth apgs
#     FAFA (Finsler): S ⊆ F — based on Finsler's extensional graphs
#     BAFA (Boffa): F ⊆ B — based on superuniversality (maximal universe)
#   In BAFA, distinct Quine atoms form a proper class.
#   This package formalizes AFA (the most widely used variant).
#
# Applications:
#   - Process algebra: modeling non-terminating concurrent processes
#   - Final semantics / coinduction in computer science
#   - Situation theory and natural language semantics
#   - Philosophical logic: liar paradox, circular propositions
#   - Non-standard analysis (Ballard & Hrbacek 1992)
#
# Known Independence / Undecidable Results:
#   - Foundation vs. AFA: independent of ZF - Foundation
#   - AFA vs. SAFA vs. FAFA vs. BAFA: mutually exclusive extensions
#   - Existence of multiple Quine atoms: consistent in BAFA, refuted in AFA
#   - Regularity is independent of ZF - Foundation (Bernays 1954, Specker 1957)
#
# Author: Lv-00 Axiom Package System (AI-Discovered)
# Date: 2026-05-28
# Version: 1.0.0
# =============================================================================

axiom "non_well_founded_set_theory" "1.0.0" {

    # =================================================================
    # Constraint Templates
    # =================================================================
    # Each template represents a fundamental axiom, operation, or
    # derived construction in ZFA (ZF with AFA replacing Regularity).
    #
    # Parameter semantics:
    #   Params = hypersets (elements of the extended universe V_AFA)
    #
    # Templates are organized into groups reflecting the structure
    # of the theory: core axioms, graph-theoretic machinery, core
    # constructors, coinductive operations, and comparison with ZFC.
    # =================================================================

    # --- Group I: Core Axioms (ZF minus Regularity, plus AFA) ---

    # Axiom of Extensionality (strengthened by AFA):
    #   Two hypersets are equal iff they have exactly the same elements.
    #   Under AFA, this is equivalent to bisimulation equality.
    #   ∀x∀y[∀z(z∈x ↔ z∈y) → x=y]
    # Params: hyperset x, hyperset y
    template "extensionality" 2

    # Anti-Foundation Axiom (AFA) — Decoration Form:
    #   Every accessible pointed graph (apg) has a unique decoration.
    #   An apg is a directed graph with a distinguished root node such
    #   that every node is reachable from the root via a finite path.
    #   A decoration assigns to each node n a hyperset d(n) such that
    #   d(n) = {d(m) : there is an edge from n to m}.
    # Params: graph G, root node r -> unique decoration d
    template "afa_decoration" 2

    # Anti-Foundation Axiom (AFA) — Solution Lemma Form:
    #   Every system of equations of the form
    #     x_i = {x_j : j ∈ I_i}  for each i in an index set I
    #   has a unique solution (up to isomorphism of the index set).
    #   This is the most practically useful formulation of AFA.
    # Params: equation system E -> unique solution assignment S
    template "solution_lemma" 1

    # Axiom Schema of Specification (Separation): [retained from ZF]
    #   ∀z∀w₁…∀wₙ∃y∀x[x∈y ↔ (x∈z ∧ φ(x,w₁,…,wₙ))]
    # Params: hyperset z, predicate φ, parameter w -> hyperset {x∈z : φ(x,w)}
    template "specification" 3

    # Axiom of Pairing: [retained from ZF]
    #   ∀x∀y∃z[x∈z ∧ y∈z]
    # Params: hyperset x, hyperset y -> hyperset {x, y}
    template "pairing" 2

    # Axiom of Union: [retained from ZF]
    #   ∀F∃A∀Y∀x[(x∈Y ∧ Y∈F) → x∈A]
    # Params: family F -> hyperset ∪F
    template "union" 1

    # Axiom Schema of Replacement: [retained from ZF]
    #   ∀A∀w₁…∀wₙ[∀x(x∈A → ∃!y φ(x,y,w₁,…,wₙ))
    #     → ∃B∀y(y∈B ↔ ∃x(x∈A ∧ φ(x,y,w₁,…,wₙ)))]
    # Params: hyperset A, functional predicate φ, parameter w -> hyperset B
    template "replacement" 3

    # Axiom of Infinity: [retained from ZF]
    #   ∃I[∅∈I ∧ ∀x(x∈I → x∪{x}∈I)]
    # Params: (none) -> hyperset ω
    template "infinity" 0

    # Axiom of Power Set: [retained from ZF]
    #   ∀x∃y∀z[z⊆x → z∈y]
    # Params: hyperset x -> hyperset P(x)
    template "power_set" 1

    # Axiom of Choice: [retained from ZFC]
    #   ∀X[∅∉X → ∃f:X→∪X such that ∀A∈X, f(A)∈A]
    # Params: family X -> choice function f
    template "choice" 1

    # --- Group II: Graph-Theoretic Machinery ---

    # Accessible Pointed Graph (apg) Construction:
    #   Construct an apg from a set of nodes and edges.
    #   An apg is a directed graph (N, E) with a distinguished root r
    #   such that every n ∈ N is reachable from r via a finite path.
    # Params: node set N, edge set E, root r -> apg (N, E, r)
    template "accessible_pointed_graph" 3

    # Graph Decoration:
    #   Given an apg, compute its unique decoration (guaranteed by AFA).
    #   The decoration maps each node to a hyperset where node n maps to
    #   the set of decorations of n's children.
    # Params: apg G -> decoration function d: N → V_AFA
    template "graph_decoration" 1

    # Bisimulation Check:
    #   Two hypersets a, b are bisimilar iff there exists a relation R
    #   such that aRb and for every x∈a there exists y∈b with xRy,
    #   and for every y∈b there exists x∈a with xRy.
    #   Under AFA, bisimilarity is equivalent to equality.
    # Params: hyperset a, hyperset b -> bisimilarity check result
    template "bisimulation_check" 2

    # Bisimulation Construction (Greatest Bisimulation):
    #   Compute the greatest bisimulation relation between two apgs.
    #   This is the union of all bisimulation relations.
    # Params: apg G1, apg G2 -> bisimulation relation R
    template "greatest_bisimulation" 2

    # Graph Isomorphism:
    #   Check whether two apgs are isomorphic (structure-preserving
    #   bijection between nodes preserving edges and the root).
    # Params: apg G1, apg G2 -> isomorphism check result
    template "graph_isomorphism" 2

    # Extensional Graph:
    #   A graph is extensional iff distinct nodes have distinct sets
    #   of children. AFA requires apgs to be extensional for unique
    #   decoration (up to isomorphism).
    # Params: graph G -> extensionality check result
    template "extensional_graph_check" 1

    # Picture of a Hyperset:
    #   Given a hyperset a, construct its canonical picture — the
    #   unique (up to isomorphism) apg that decorates to {a}.
    # Params: hyperset a -> canonical apg picture
    template "picture_of_hyperset" 1

    # --- Group III: Core Constructors (Hyperset Operations) ---

    # Empty Hyperset: ∅ = {x : x ≠ x} (same as in ZFC)
    # Params: (none) -> hyperset ∅
    template "empty_set" 0

    # Singleton: {a} (same as in ZFC)
    # Params: hyperset a -> hyperset {a}
    template "singleton" 1

    # Quine Atom: the unique hyperset Ω such that Ω = {Ω}
    #   Under AFA, the equation x = {x} has exactly one solution.
    #   This is the simplest non-well-founded set.
    # Params: (none) -> hyperset Ω
    template "quine_atom" 0

    # Non-Well-Founded Singleton Chain:
    #   Construct the unique hyperset satisfying a₀ = {a₁}, a₁ = {a₂}, ...
    #   This is a finite or infinite descending chain of singletons.
    # Params: chain length n (0 = Quine atom, 1 = {∅}, etc.) -> hyperset chain
    template "singleton_chain" 1

    # Mutual Recursion Pair:
    #   Construct the unique pair (a, b) satisfying a = {b}, b = {a}.
    #   Under AFA, this system has a unique solution.
    # Params: (none) -> pair of hypersets (a, b)
    template "mutual_recursion_pair" 0

    # Equation System Solver:
    #   Given a system of set equations, compute the unique solution
    #   using the Solution Lemma (AFA).
    #   System format: {x_i = {x_j : j ∈ I_i} : i ∈ I}
    # Params: index set I, right-hand side map -> solution assignment
    template "solve_equation_system" 2

    # Ordered Pair (Kuratowski): (a,b) = {{a}, {a,b}}
    # Params: hyperset a, hyperset b -> hyperset (a,b)
    template "ordered_pair" 2

    # Cartesian Product: A × B
    # Params: hyperset A, hyperset B -> hyperset A×B
    template "cartesian_product" 2

    # Binary Union: A ∪ B
    # Params: hyperset A, hyperset B -> hyperset A∪B
    template "binary_union" 2

    # Binary Intersection: A ∩ B
    # Params: hyperset A, hyperset B -> hyperset A∩B
    template "binary_intersection" 2

    # Set Difference: A \ B
    # Params: hyperset A, hyperset B -> hyperset A\B
    template "set_difference" 2

    # Subset Check: A ⊆ B
    # Params: hyperset A, hyperset B -> check result
    template "subset_relation" 2

    # Big Intersection: ∩F
    # Params: family F -> hyperset ∩F
    template "big_intersection" 1

    # --- Group IV: Coinductive Operations ---

    # Greatest Fixed Point (ν):
    #   Given a monotone operator Γ on hypersets, compute the greatest
    #   fixed point νΓ = ∪{X : X ⊆ Γ(X)}. This is the coinductive
    #   counterpart to the least fixed point μΓ.
    #   In ZFA, greatest fixed points always exist for monotone operators
    #   on the universe of hypersets.
    # Params: monotone operator Γ -> hyperset νΓ
    template "greatest_fixed_point" 1

    # Coreflexive Coinductive Definition:
    #   Define a hyperset as the greatest fixed point of a system of
    #   equations. Used for modeling streams, infinite trees, processes.
    # Params: system of guarded equations -> hyperset (coinductive definition)
    template "coinductive_definition" 1

    # Stream (Infinite Sequence):
    #   Construct an infinite stream ⟨a₀, a₁, a₂, ...⟩ as a hyperset
    #   satisfying s = (a₀, s') where s' = (a₁, s''), etc.
    #   Streams are a canonical example of non-well-founded sets.
    # Params: head element a, tail stream s -> stream (a :: s)
    template "stream_cons" 2

    # Stream Unfolding:
    #   Given a function f: X → X × A and initial state x₀, construct
    #   the stream ⟨a₀, a₁, a₂, ...⟩ where f(xₙ) = (xₙ₊₁, aₙ).
    # Params: function f, initial state x₀ -> stream
    template "stream_unfold" 2

    # Labeled Transition System (LTS):
    #   Model a process as a hyperset of (action, next-state) pairs.
    #   This is the standard representation for process algebra in ZFA.
    # Params: state set S, transition relation T -> LTS as hyperset
    template "lts_construction" 2

    # Bisimulation on LTS:
    #   Check whether two labeled transition systems are bisimilar.
    #   Two processes are bisimilar iff they cannot be distinguished
    #   by any finite sequence of observations.
    # Params: LTS P, LTS Q -> bisimilarity check result
    template "lts_bisimulation" 2

    # --- Group V: Comparison with ZFC ---

    # Well-Founded Part:
    #   The class WF of well-founded sets is the largest subclass of
    #   V_AFA satisfying the Axiom of Regularity. WF coincides with
    #   the von Neumann cumulative hierarchy V.
    #   Every theorem of ZFC that does not depend on Regularity holds
    #   in WF ⊆ V_AFA.
    # Params: hyperset a -> check if a ∈ WF
    template "well_founded_check" 1

    # Rank Function (for well-founded part):
    #   For well-founded sets, the rank function assigns an ordinal
    #   rank(a) measuring the depth of nesting. Non-well-founded sets
    #   have no rank (rank is undefined).
    # Params: hyperset a -> ordinal rank(a) or undefined
    template "rank_function" 1

    # Foundation Violation Check:
    #   Check whether a hyperset violates the Axiom of Regularity,
    #   i.e., whether there exists an infinite descending ∈-chain
    #   starting from a.
    # Params: hyperset a -> check result
    template "foundation_violation_check" 1

    # Membership Cycle Detection:
    #   Check whether a hyperset a participates in a finite ∈-cycle,
    #   i.e., a ∈ a₁ ∈ ... ∈ aₙ ∈ a for some n ≥ 1.
    # Params: hyperset a -> cycle detection result
    template "membership_cycle_detection" 1

    # ZFC Embedding:
    #   The well-founded part WF of V_AFA is isomorphic to the full
    #   ZFC universe V. This template represents the embedding map.
    #   All ZFC theorems hold when relativized to WF.
    # Params: (none) -> embedding witness
    template "zfc_embedding" 0

    # --- Group VI: Relation & Function Constructors ---

    # Relation: R ⊆ A × B (same as ZFC)
    # Params: hyperset A, hyperset B, predicate R -> relation R
    template "relation" 3

    # Function: f: A → B (same as ZFC)
    # Params: hyperset A, hyperset B, graph f -> function f
    template "function" 3

    # Function Application: f(x)
    # Params: function f, hyperset x -> value f(x)
    template "function_application" 2

    # Image: f[A] = {f(x) : x ∈ A}
    # Params: function f, hyperset A -> image f[A]
    template "image" 2

    # Inverse Image: f⁻¹[B] = {x : f(x) ∈ B}
    # Params: function f, hyperset B -> inverse image f⁻¹[B]
    template "inverse_image" 2

    # --- Group VII: Cardinal & Ordinal Constructors ---

    # Equinumerosity: A ≈ B
    # Params: hyperset A, hyperset B -> check result
    template "equinumerous" 2

    # Cardinality: |A|
    # Params: hyperset A -> cardinal |A|
    template "cardinality" 1

    # Well-Order: well-ordering on a set
    # Params: hyperset A, relation < -> well-order
    template "well_order" 2

    # =================================================================
    # Known Unconstructible / Undecidable Problems
    # =================================================================

    # 1. Foundation vs. AFA Consistency
    # The Axiom of Regularity (Foundation) is independent of ZF minus
    # Foundation. Both ZF+Foundation and ZF+AFA are consistent (relative
    # to ZF). Neither implies the other.
    # (Bernays 1954, Specker 1957)
    unconstructible "foundation_independence" {
        reduces_to "independent_of_ZF_minus_foundation"
        dependency "extensionality"
        dependency "afa_decoration"
        dependency "solution_lemma"
        dependency "well_founded_check"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_regularity"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 2. AFA vs. SAFA vs. FAFA vs. BAFA Mutual Exclusivity
    # The four major anti-foundation axioms (AFA, SAFA, FAFA, BAFA)
    # are mutually exclusive. No two can hold simultaneously.
    # The universes satisfy V ⊆ A ⊆ S ⊆ F ⊆ B.
    unconstructible "afa_safa_fafa_bafa_exclusivity" {
        reduces_to "mutually_exclusive_extensions_of_ZF"
        dependency "afa_decoration"
        dependency "extensional_graph_check"
        dependency "graph_isomorphism"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-well-founded_set_theory"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 3. Uniqueness of Quine Atom under AFA
    # Under AFA, the equation x = {x} has exactly one solution (the
    # unique Quine atom Ω). Under BAFA, there is a proper class of
    # distinct Quine atoms. The uniqueness is provable in ZFA+AFA.
    unconstructible "quine_atom_uniqueness_in_bafa" {
        reduces_to "refuted_in_boffa_universe"
        dependency "quine_atom"
        dependency "solution_lemma"
        dependency "bisimulation_check"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quine_atom"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 4. Consistency of ZFA relative to ZFC
    # ZFA (ZF + AFA) is consistent if and only if ZFC is consistent.
    # This follows from the fact that ZFA has a model constructed
    # inside ZFC using graph-theoretic techniques.
    unconstructible "zfa_consistency" {
        reduces_to "equiconsistent_with_ZFC"
        dependency "afa_decoration"
        dependency "accessible_pointed_graph"
        dependency "graph_decoration"
        external_ref "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/nonwellfounded-set-theory/"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 5. Existence of Non-Well-Founded Sets in ZFC
    # ZFC (with Foundation) proves that no non-well-founded sets exist.
    # The existence of hypersets is refutable in ZFC.
    unconstructible "hyperset_existence_in_zfc" {
        reduces_to "refutable_in_ZFC"
        dependency "foundation_violation_check"
        dependency "membership_cycle_detection"
        dependency "well_founded_check"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_regularity"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 6. Bisimulation Decidability for Finite Hypersets
    # Checking bisimilarity of two finite hypersets (given as finite
    # graphs) is decidable in polynomial time. However, checking
    # bisimilarity of infinite-state systems is undecidable in general.
    unconstructible "bisimulation_undecidable_infinite" {
        reduces_to "undecidable_for_infinite_state_systems"
        dependency "bisimulation_check"
        dependency "greatest_bisimulation"
        dependency "lts_bisimulation"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bisimulation"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 7. Continuum Hypothesis in ZFA
    # CH remains independent of ZFA (since ZFA extends ZF and does
    # not resolve CH). The independence proof from ZFC carries over.
    unconstructible "continuum_hypothesis_in_zfa" {
        reduces_to "independent_of_ZFA"
        dependency "infinity"
        dependency "power_set"
        dependency "cardinality"
        dependency "zfc_embedding"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continuum_hypothesis"
        green_verified true
    }

    # 8. Axiom of Choice in ZFA
    # AC is independent of ZF+AFA (same as in ZF). There are models
    # of ZFA where AC holds and models where it fails.
    unconstructible "choice_independence_in_zfa" {
        reduces_to "independent_of_ZF_plus_AFA"
        dependency "choice"
        dependency "well_order"
        dependency "zfc_embedding"
        external_ref "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_choice#Independence"
        green_verified true
    }

    # =================================================================
    # Bottom Geometry & Logical Framework
    # =================================================================
    #
    # bottom_geometry: "hyperset_graph_universe"
    #   ⊥ (contradiction) in ZFA is represented as the empty hyperset ∅,
    #   which is the unique well-founded set with no elements. However,
    #   the "geometry" of ZFA is fundamentally graph-theoretic: every
    #   hyperset is uniquely determined by its canonical picture (an
    #   accessible pointed graph). The universe V_AFA is the class of
    #   all decorations of all apgs. This graph-theoretic perspective
    #   is the natural "bottom geometry" for non-well-founded sets,
    #   as it replaces the cumulative hierarchy V with the universe
    #   of all apg decorations.
    #
    # negation_encoding: "classical_complement_in_hyperset_universe"
    #   ¬φ is encoded as the complement of the extension of φ within
    #   the universe of hypersets V_AFA. Since ZFA uses classical
    #   first-order logic (same as ZFC), negation is classical and
    #   satisfies the law of excluded middle: φ ∨ ¬φ is always true.
    #
    # contradiction_behavior: "explosion_principle"
    #   ZFA uses classical logic with the principle of explosion
    #   (ex contradictione quodlibet): from ⊥, anything follows.
    #   This is the same as in ZFC, since ZFA retains classical logic.
    # =================================================================

    bottom_geometry "hyperset_graph_universe"
    negation_encoding "classical_complement_in_hyperset_universe"
    contradiction_behavior "explosion_principle"
}
